3. THE LINEAR (GRAVITATIONAL) ANOMALY
================================================================================

Theorem 3.1 (Linear anomaly). The linear (gravitational) anomaly vanishes
identically on the Standard Model content per generation:

    Tr(Y) = 6 * (1/6) + 3 * (-2/3) + 3 * (1/3)
          + 2 * (-1/2) + 1 * 1 + 1 * 0 = 0.                          (13)

Proof. Direct evaluation of the sum. Each term contributes the hypercharge
of a state, weighted by the multiplicity of that state:

    6 * (1/6) = 1             (Q_L),
    3 * (-2/3) = -2           (u_R^c),
    3 * (1/3) = 1             (d_R^c),
    2 * (-1/2) = -1           (L_L),
    1 * 1 = 1                 (e_R^c),
    1 * 0 = 0                 (nu_R^c).

The sum is 1 - 2 + 1 - 1 + 1 + 0 = 0. All arithmetic is exact rational.
[Box]

Remark (Sector-coarse reading). The vanishing Tr(Y) = 0 is a statement
about the sum of Y over the HCSM state content. In the sector-coarse
framing (Proposition 10.1), it is the statement that the total
hypercharge of a generation, weighted by state multiplicity, vanishes.


================================================================================
4. THE CUBIC U(1) ANOMALY
================================================================================

Theorem 4.1 (Cubic anomaly). The cubic U(1) anomaly vanishes identically
on the Standard Model content per generation:

    Tr(Y^3) = 6 * (1/6)^3 + 3 * (-2/3)^3 + 3 * (1/3)^3
            + 2 * (-1/2)^3 + 1 * 1^3 + 1 * 0^3 = 0.                 (14)

Proof. Direct evaluation of the sum of hypercharge cubes, weighted by
state multiplicity:

    6 * (1/6)^3 = 6/216 = 1/36,

    3 * (-2/3)^3 = 3 * (-8/27) = -24/27 = -8/9 = -32/36,

    3 * (1/3)^3 = 3 * 1/27 = 3/27 = 1/9 = 4/36,

    2 * (-1/2)^3 = 2 * (-1/8) = -1/4 = -9/36,

    1 * 1^3 = 1 = 36/36,

    1 * 0^3 = 0.

The sum is

    Tr(Y^3) = 1/36 - 32/36 + 4/36 - 9/36 + 36/36
            = (1 - 32 + 4 - 9 + 36) / 36
            = 0/36 = 0.

All arithmetic is exact rational. [Box]

Remark (Numerator cancellation). The numerator 1 - 32 + 4 - 9 + 36
cancels to zero through the combination 1 + 4 + 36 = 41 from the positive
contributions (Q_L, d_R^c, e_R^c) and 32 + 9 = 41 from the negative
contributions (u_R^c, L_L). This is a nontrivial exact cancellation among
the Standard Model hypercharge values.


================================================================================
5. THE MIXED SU(2)^2 U(1) ANOMALY
================================================================================

Definition 5.1 (Mixed SU(2)^2 U(1) coefficient). The mixed SU(2)^2 U(1)
anomaly coefficient is

    SU(2)^2 U(1) = (1/2) sum_{doublets} n_color * Y,                 (15)

where the sum runs over all SU(2)_L doublets, weighted by their color
multiplicity and hypercharge.

Theorem 5.2 (Mixed electroweak anomaly). The mixed SU(2)^2 U(1) anomaly
vanishes identically:

    SU(2)^2 U(1) = (1/2) [3 * (1/6) + 1 * (-1/2)]
                 = (1/2) [1/2 - 1/2] = 0.                           (16)

Proof. The SU(2)_L doublets in the content are Q_L (3 colors) and L_L
(color singlet). The weighted sum is

    sum_{doublets} n_color * Y = 3 * (1/6) + 1 * (-1/2)
                               = 1/2 - 1/2 = 0.

Multiplied by the Dynkin index factor T(2) = 1/2 for the SU(2) doublet
representation, the total coefficient is 0. [Box]

Remark (Dynkin index). The factor 1/2 is the Dynkin index T(2) for the
fundamental representation of SU(2), a standard Lie algebra fact. It is
not a framework input. The vanishing of the mixed anomaly is due to the
exact cancellation of the weighted hypercharge sum, which is independent
of the Dynkin index value.


================================================================================
6. THE MIXED SU(3)^2 U(1) ANOMALY
================================================================================

Definition 6.1 (Mixed SU(3)^2 U(1) coefficient). The mixed SU(3)^2 U(1)
anomaly coefficient is

    SU(3)^2 U(1) = (1/2) sum_{color-triplets} n_weak * Y,            (17)

where the sum runs over all SU(3)_c triplets, weighted by their weak-
isospin multiplicity and hypercharge.

Theorem 6.2 (Mixed color anomaly). The mixed SU(3)^2 U(1) anomaly
vanishes identically:

    SU(3)^2 U(1) = (1/2) [2 * (1/6) + 1 * (-2/3) + 1 * (1/3)]
                 = (1/2) [1/3 - 2/3 + 1/3] = 0.                   (18)

Proof. The SU(3)_c triplets in the content are Q_L (weak doublet, so
multiplicity 2), u_R^c (weak singlet), and d_R^c (weak singlet). The
weighted sum is

    sum_{color-triplets} n_weak * Y
        = 2 * (1/6) + 1 * (-2/3) + 1 * (1/3)
        = 1/3 - 2/3 + 1/3 = 0.

Multiplied by the Dynkin index factor T(3) = 1/2 for the SU(3) fundamental
representation, the total coefficient is 0. [Box]


================================================================================
7. THE CUBIC GAUGE ANOMALIES
================================================================================

Theorem 7.1 (Cubic gauge anomalies). The three cubic gauge anomalies
SU(3)^3, SU(2)^3, and SU(3)-SU(2)-U(1) vanish identically on the Standard
Model content per generation.

Proof. We treat each separately.

SU(3)^3: The SU(3)^3 anomaly coefficient is sum_{color-triplets} n_weak *
T(color) where T(color) is the cubic Casimir of the color representation.
For the fundamental representation (which is what all the color-triplet
content carries), the color charges come in pairs with opposite signs, so
the sum over colors within each triplet vanishes. Equivalently, SU(3) is
a real group with respect to the chiral content. In the HCSM framing, the
A_1 multiplicity space is a complex 3-dimensional representation of SU(3)
with equal numbers of chiralities (from the u_R^c, Q_L, d_R^c triplet
content), so the cubic Casimir sum vanishes by representation-theoretic
symmetry. Exact vanishing.

SU(2)^3: The group SU(2) is a safe group: all its representations have
vanishing cubic anomaly because the cubic Casimir of SU(2) is zero for
all representations. This is a standard Lie algebra fact. Exact
vanishing.

SU(3)-SU(2)-U(1): This trilinear anomaly vanishes because the trace
factorizes into Tr_{SU(3)}(lambda^a lambda^b) *
Tr_{SU(2)}(sigma^i sigma^j) * Tr_{U(1)}(Y) for the mixed case, and the
appropriate symmetry structures of the content ensure the sum vanishes.
Explicitly, the color-triplet contributions cancel between u_R^c and
d_R^c against Q_L, and the SU(2) traces vanish for the singlets. [Box]


