8. THE WITTEN GLOBAL ANOMALY
================================================================================

Definition 8.1 (Witten global anomaly). The Witten global SU(2) anomaly
is a Z2-valued global anomaly associated with the fourth homotopy group
pi_4(SU(2)) = Z2. A theory with chiral SU(2) content is globally
consistent only if the number of SU(2) doublets is even.

Theorem 8.2 (Witten anomaly evasion). The Witten global SU(2) anomaly is
evaded: each generation of the Standard Model content carries an even
number of SU(2)_L doublets.

Proof. The SU(2)_L doublets in the content are Q_L and L_L:

    Q_L: 3 colors x 1 doublet = 3 doublet states,

    L_L: 1 color singlet x 1 doublet = 1 doublet state.

Total: 3 + 1 = 4 doublets per generation, which is even. The global SU(2)
anomaly is therefore evaded. [Box]

Remark (HCSM framing). In HCSM terms, the number of SU(2)_L doublets is
the multiplicity of the B_1 isotypic content that participates in the
weak interaction. The even count is a structural feature of the Standard
Model content as realized on the HCSM A_1 and B_1 multiplicity spaces.


================================================================================
9. TWO-LOOP AUTOMATICITY
================================================================================

Theorem 9.1 (Two-loop automatic). By the Adler-Bardeen theorem, if the
one-loop anomaly coefficients vanish, then all higher-loop anomaly
coefficients vanish. In particular, the two-loop anomaly cancels
automatically.

Proof. The Adler-Bardeen theorem states that the ABJ anomaly (the anomaly
of the axial current) is one-loop exact: it receives no corrections at
higher loops. Consequently, the anomaly coefficient A satisfies
A = A_{one-loop} at all orders. Since the one-loop coefficients vanish by
Theorems 3.1-7.1, the anomaly vanishes at all loops. [Box]

Remark (Wess-Zumino consistency). The Wess-Zumino consistency conditions
relate the anomaly coefficients across different orders. When the
one-loop coefficients vanish, the Wess-Zumino conditions are trivially
satisfied at all orders. The two-loop anomaly is therefore automatically
zero, with no additional constraints on the Standard Model content.


