10. SECTOR-COARSE FRAMING
================================================================================

The anomaly conditions have a natural expression in the sector-coarse
language of HCSM-06 [HCSM-06] and HCSM-07 [HCSM-07].

Proposition 10.1 (Sector-coarse identity for the mixed color anomaly). On
the A_1 isotypic block, the mixed SU(3)^2 U(1) anomaly takes the
sector-coarse form

    Y_0 + 2 Y_1 + Y_2 = 0,                                           (19)

where Y_0, Y_1, Y_2 are the hypercharge values on the sectors
Omega^0, Omega^1, Omega^2.

Proof. The mixed SU(3)^2 U(1) coefficient (17) is

    SU(3)^2 U(1) = (1/2) [n_weak(u_R^c) * Y_0
                        + n_weak(Q_L) * Y_1
                        + n_weak(d_R^c) * Y_2],

where the weak multiplicities are n_weak(u_R^c) = 1 (singlet),
n_weak(Q_L) = 2 (doublet), n_weak(d_R^c) = 1 (singlet). The sector
assignment (Input 3 of HCSM-07) places u_R^c on Omega^0, Q_L on Omega^1,
d_R^c on Omega^2.

Substituting:

    SU(3)^2 U(1) = (1/2) [Y_0 + 2 Y_1 + Y_2].

The vanishing of this quantity is equivalent to Y_0 + 2 Y_1 + Y_2 = 0.
With the HCSM values Y_0 = -2/3, Y_1 = +1/6, Y_2 = +1/3 (HCSM-07
[HCSM-07]), the weighted sum is

    -2/3 + 2 * (1/6) + 1/3 = -2/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 = 0.

[Box]

Corollary 10.2 (Sector-coarse reading of anomaly cancellation). In the
sector-coarse framing, the mixed SU(3)^2 U(1) anomaly condition is a
specific weighted sum of hypercharge values over the three Hodge sectors:

    Y(Omega^0) + 2 Y(Omega^1) + Y(Omega^2) = 0.

The weight on Omega^1 is 2, reflecting the fact that the middle sector
hosts the weak doublet content (Q_L); the outer sectors Omega^0 and
Omega^2 host singlet content with weight 1 each.

Remark (Sector-coarse content of the other conditions). The other three
anomaly conditions admit analogous sector-coarse readings:

  - Tr(Y) = 0: the weighted sum over all states of Y vanishes.
  - Tr(Y^3) = 0: the weighted sum over all states of Y^3 vanishes.
  - SU(2)^2 U(1) = 0: the weighted sum over the weak-doublet content
    vanishes.

In each case, the vanishing is due to an exact rational cancellation
among the HCSM hypercharge values, reflecting the anomaly-free structure
of the HCSM hypercharge functional.


